WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.944 --> 00:00:02.394
- [Narrator] I think the
average person imagines

00:00:02.394 --> 00:00:06.700
a pirate with hook for a
hand, a great big, floppy hat,

00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:11.310
blue waistcoats, and maybe even a peg leg,

00:00:11.310 --> 00:00:12.174
parrot on his shoulder.
(parrot squawks)

00:00:12.174 --> 00:00:16.810
- Yargh.
(upbeat sea shanty music)

00:00:16.810 --> 00:00:19.690
- [Narrator] This particular
gang of pirates were active

00:00:19.690 --> 00:00:21.810
in the very early part of the 18th century

00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:23.720
for only four or five years,

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:26.446
but they included Blackbeard,
Black Sam Bellamy,

00:00:26.446 --> 00:00:30.220
Stede Bonnet, the women pirates
Mary Read and Anne Bonny,

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:33.210
were all part of this one
gang, and it's this gang

00:00:33.210 --> 00:00:37.210
that all of the pop culture
images and iconography

00:00:37.210 --> 00:00:39.190
relating to pirates comes from.

00:00:39.190 --> 00:00:42.020
One of the most common
oceanic sailing routes

00:00:42.020 --> 00:00:45.990
for English seamen was to go
from England to the Caribbean

00:00:45.990 --> 00:00:49.300
because that was a major
route of merchant shipping,

00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:53.510
and so they would come back
to gray, dreary England

00:00:53.510 --> 00:00:55.700
and one of the things they
would bring with them often

00:00:55.700 --> 00:00:58.320
were these colorful
birds from the tropics,

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:01.260
especially parrots who
could be trained to sit

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:05.010
on your shoulder but also
to actually speak words.

00:01:05.010 --> 00:01:07.180
You can imagine how
boring it must have been

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:09.740
on your off hours, what
are you going to do?

00:01:09.740 --> 00:01:13.630
The colorful and talkative
parrots were treasured

00:01:13.630 --> 00:01:16.780
both as a form of
entertainment and recreation,

00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:19.730
all of the characteristics pirates had,

00:01:19.730 --> 00:01:22.760
because, you know,
having a parrot as a pet,

00:01:22.760 --> 00:01:26.200
missing arms, eyes, and hands and so forth

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:29.990
was very common amongst
sailors in that time period.

00:01:29.990 --> 00:01:33.310
Most pirates had been
merchant or naval sailors

00:01:33.310 --> 00:01:34.840
prior to going into piracy,

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:38.190
and the big piracy outbreak
was prompted by the ending

00:01:38.190 --> 00:01:41.190
of a big colonial war, the
War of Spanish Succession.

00:01:41.190 --> 00:01:43.490
You had thousands of people without means

00:01:43.490 --> 00:01:47.710
to sustain themselves and
with a special skill set.

00:01:47.710 --> 00:01:49.430
The pressures on sailors were enormous,

00:01:49.430 --> 00:01:53.840
so many of them mutinied
and took over their vessels.

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:57.160
These pirates from the
Golden Age of Piracy

00:01:57.160 --> 00:02:00.230
were folk heroes at the
time they were still alive.

00:02:00.230 --> 00:02:02.940
They argued they weren't
thieves and brigands.

00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:04.430
They said that they were engaged

00:02:04.430 --> 00:02:06.200
in essentially a social revolt

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:08.120
against the ship captains and ship owners

00:02:08.120 --> 00:02:10.710
who'd made ordinary
sailors' lives miserable,

00:02:10.710 --> 00:02:13.340
that they were Robin
Hood figures, you know,

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:16.130
robbing from the wicked
rich to settle scores.

00:02:16.130 --> 00:02:18.020
Many of them had been in accidents

00:02:18.020 --> 00:02:20.890
because being a sailor,
whether a pirate or not,

00:02:20.890 --> 00:02:23.410
aboard a ship was a very
dangerous proposition.

00:02:23.410 --> 00:02:27.400
Rigging fell in storms, cargo
in barrels shifted around.

00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:29.570
In battle you would be
hit by wood splinters

00:02:29.570 --> 00:02:31.050
or who knows what else,

00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:34.240
so lots and lots of pirates
and sailors also may have had

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:38.610
eye patches and peg legs and
hooks or other things for arms

00:02:38.610 --> 00:02:41.870
because they'd lost their
eyes or limbs (laughs)

00:02:41.870 --> 00:02:44.630
in these various accidents or in combat.

00:02:44.630 --> 00:02:46.710
So the pirates probably
looked a little bit like

00:02:46.710 --> 00:02:49.740
some crazy scene from, you
know, the early Mad Max movies,

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:51.940
a bunch of people bizarrely dressed

00:02:51.940 --> 00:02:54.240
like a post-apocalyptic image,

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:56.880
and you know that was precisely the idea,

00:02:56.880 --> 00:02:59.080
especially Blackbeard's, was

00:02:59.080 --> 00:03:02.310
to cultivate a image of terror

00:03:02.310 --> 00:03:04.330
so that everyone would
be so scared of them

00:03:04.330 --> 00:03:06.393
they'd surrender without a fight,

00:03:07.250 --> 00:03:08.780
but, in all the accounts of all

00:03:08.780 --> 00:03:11.590
of Blackbeard's raids
and captures of ships,

00:03:11.590 --> 00:03:12.800
he threatens a lot of people,

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:15.820
but he never actually kills anybody

00:03:15.820 --> 00:03:19.333
prior to his final, fatal
battle with the Royal Navy.

